==========================================
DAY 26 NOTES — Regular Expressions in Linux
Date: 24-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- . matches ANY character; \. matches a LITERAL dot (escaping matters!)
- + = one or more, ? = zero or one, {n} = exactly n times — all need grep -E
- [0-9] = any digit, [a-z] = any lowercase letter, [^0-9] = NOT a digit
- grep -E (extended regex) avoids the need to escape +, ?, {} — almost always use -E
- grep -Eo extracts JUST the matched text, not the whole line
- Combining a regex pattern + sort -u = list of UNIQUE matches (great for IP extraction)
- Today's IP pattern is simplified — real validation regex is more complex

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] grep with character classes [abc], [0-9]
[ ] grep -E with +, ?, {}
[ ] grep -Eo (extracting matches)
[ ] building an IP address pattern
[ ] sed -E for pattern-based replacement

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ echo "Order #45231" | grep -Eo "[0-9]+"
> 

$ grep -Eo "[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}" /var/log/auth.log | sort -u
> 

$ echo "Contact: 9876543210" | sed -E 's/[0-9]{10}/[REDACTED]/'
> 


